On Sep 16, 1:43 am, Martin Read <mpr...@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:
> James <james.ka...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >Independently of the other issues, this raises a point I've
> >been vaguely wondering about.  As far as I know, nethack uses
> >the C library rand() functions (or perhaps random() or some
> >such on platforms where it is available).

> Nethack has several options for which RNG it uses, and at
> least some versions of Nethack actually *shipped* with a copy
> of the Berkeley random() in the source archive because so many
> Unix systems had the canonical lame crippled^W^W linear
> congruential RNG as rand(). (Linux's libc uses Berkeley
> random() as rand(), since the C standard makes no direct
> requirements of the algorithm used by rand().)

> (If you wawnt to see why LCRNGs are useless for this sort of thing, see
>    http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_congruential_generator
> and its pretty picture of how triples of consecutive results get
> distributed.)

Which may not be relevant here.  A good linear congruential
generator (e.g. Park-Miller) should be largely sufficient for
our purposes.

In fact, that was part of what was behind my posting.  What
would be the effects of a poor generator?  Suppose that you
simply implemented the generator:

    unsigned current =3D 0 ;

    int
    rand()
    {
        return ++ current & 0x7FFF ;
    }

Horribly unrandom, but would it make any real difference on game
play?  (My suspicions are that it would more or less eliminate
"exceptional" cases, like no potions of healing.)

> Really, it would make most sense to just drag in the Mersenne
> Twister and have done with it; the game will have identical
> randomness on all platforms. (Crawl did this and it fixed a
> severe problem with item generation on the Windows version
> which led to gold being cripplingly rare outside of the Orcish
> Mines.)

I'd sort of favor something along those lines as well.  (The
Mersenne twister requires a bit of memory, so might cause
problems for porting to things like Palms, although I think they
now have enough memory as well.  But other reasonably good
generators exist: I combine the results from two LCG's with
slightly different periods, for example, which seems to work
well, and requires significantly less memory (and probably less
runtime as well).

